://api.github.com/users/github-actions%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"created_at":"2026-10-12T11:52:05Z","updated_at":"2026-10-12T11:52:05Z","body":"## AI Security Review\n**Experiment condition:** C\n**Automated operational score:** 85/100 — LOW — APPROVE\n*This tool is for demonstration purposes only.*\n\n### Summary\nThe AI reviewer reported 1 confirmed actionable finding(s).\n\n### Findings\n- **MEDIUM · POLICY_OR_COMPLIANCE · VNet configured with public DNS resolver in violation of NET-002**\n  - Category rationale: Policy NET-002 explicitly prohibits public DNS servers on Virtual Networks and requires Azure DNS or internal DNS. The Terraform resource sets dns_servers to [\"8.8.8.8\"], a well-known public Google DNS resolver. The inline comment acknowledges this is deliberate for test case TC06, but the policy text contains no research-workload exemption for DNS server configuration, unlike NET-003 which explicitly exempts non-production workloads. Both Checkov rules CKV_AZURE_183 and CKV_AZURE_182 independently flag this resource, corroborating the finding.\n  - Evidence: azurerm_virtual_network.vnet in terraform/network.tf: dns_servers = [\"8.8.8.8\"]. The inline comment reads '# TC06: Deliberately use a public DNS resolver for testing.' Policy NET-002 states: 'Virtual Networks must NOT use public DNS servers. Use Azure DNS or internal DNS only.' Checkov CKV_AZURE_183 ('Ensure that VNET uses local DNS addresses') and CKV_AZURE_182 ('Ensure that VNET has at least 2 connected DNS Endpoints') both flag azurerm_virtual_network.vnet in /network.tf.\n  - Analysis: The azurerm_virtual_network.vnet resource sets dns_servers = [\"8.8.8.8\"], routing DNS queries for all VNet resources to a public Google resolver. Policy NET-002 mandates that Virtual Networks use Azure DNS or internal DNS only. Unlike NET-003, NET-002 contains no carve-out for research or non-production workloads. Using a public DNS resolver exposes internal name resolution traffic to a third-party service, may leak internal hostnames or query patterns externally, and prevents private DNS zone resolution from functioning correctly for the Azure Monitor Private Link Scope and associated private DNS zones configured in nsg.tf.\n  - Impact: DNS queries from resources in this VNet are forwarded to 8.8.8.8 rather than Azure's internal resolver. This breaks resolution of the five private DNS zones linked to the VNet (privatelink.monitor.azure.com, privatelink.oms.opinsights.azure.com, privatelink.ods.opinsights.azure.com, privatelink.agentsvc.azure-automation.net, privatelink.blob.core.windows.net), undermining the private endpoint path configured for Azure Monitor. It also routes potentially sensitive hostname queries to a public third-party resolver.\n  - Recommendation: Remove the dns_servers attribute entirely or set it to an empty list (dns_servers = []) to use Azure-provided DNS, which resolves private DNS zones linked to the VNet. If a specific internal forwarder is required, supply its private IP. The README describes the intended posture as 'dns_servers = [] explicitly selects Azure-provided DNS', confirming the public resolver is not an intended permanent configuration.\n\n### Policy mappings\n- **NET-002** — Virtual Networks must not use public DNS servers (finding F-001)\n\n### Recommendation\nReview and remediate the confirmed findings above.\n","author_association":"NONE","reactions":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/abdulwaheed384/ai-pr-review/issues/comments/6123706820/reactions","total_count":0,"+1":0,"-1":0,"laugh":0,"hooray":0,"confused":0,"heart":0,"rocket":0,"eyes":0},"performed_via_github_app":{"id":15368,"client_id":"Iv1.05c79e9ad1f6bdfa","slug":"github-actions","node_id":"MDM6QXBwMTUzNjg=","owner":{"login":"github","id":9919,"node_id":"MDEyOk9yZ2FuaXphdGlvbjk5MTk=","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/9919?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/github","